# Printed-page overlays and local printing

## Scope and existing evidence

Support printed-page flags and background-free overlays in the Rust compositor
with explicit printer intent, leaving physical plotting out of scope.

The historical accepted theoretical design is preserved in completed P1–P9
records. This record is **open executable integration work**. It must not be
closed merely because a prior design was accepted or a different
implementation was proposed.

## Colored ink, date and preprinted two-page spool parity — 2026-11-04

The independent fake-CUPS print test now submits exact native Rust bytes
from a two-page preprinted PDF with a validated 2026-11-04 date, page
numbers, rounded border and one colored diagonal pen segment on page two.
It verifies that suppressing existing ruling reduces paper-mark strokes
while retaining those printed annotations and page-specific ink, and that
`--dry-run` cannot contact the fake spool. Explicit submission reproduces
the PDF bytes unchanged through the fixed CUPS argument contract.

All three print tests pass, and the full notebook-paper crate now passes
strict all-target Clippy, including the previously failing test fixture.
No real local print queue was contacted; physical alignment, saved image
layers and complete notebook acceptance remain open.

## Review-locked local print bytes (partial) — 2026-11-04

The opt-in `atrament-print --expect-sha256` now compares an exact 64-digit
reviewed digest against the same bounded PDF snapshot sent to CUPS. `--dry-run`
reports SHA-256 without spawning a printer. The native FIPS SHA-256 primitive
is reused; the print adapter never reopens the pathname after checksum
validation. Fake-spool acceptance covers unchanged native two-page PDF bytes,
refusal of malformed/duplicate hashes, and refusal when a different but still
valid PDF replaces the reviewed file; no print occurs on checksum mismatch.

This is an explicit safety guard for a previously previewed PDF, not a PDF
security parser, proof of printer output, or complete saved image workflow.

## Exact PDF version-line refusal before local spool (partial) — 2026-11-04

The explicit `atrament-print` selected-file gate now requires a complete
header line with PDF 1.0–1.7 or 2.0 and a CR/LF boundary before continuing.
Malformed, truncated and unsupported-version prefixes that previously passed
its loose `%PDF-1.`/`%PDF-2.` check now refuse before CUPS launch.

The four focused native Rust tests cover the actual two-page PDF, allowed
version labels, ten malformed sources and three independent fake-spool
refusals. No rejected source file is changed, and production Clippy passes.
This remains a framing check, not a full PDF parser or physical print proof.

## Cross-reference locator refusal before local spool (partial) — 2026-11-04

The explicit `atrament-print` CLI no longer accepts a `%PDF-1.7` header and
terminal `%%EOF` as sufficient evidence of a framed PDF. The same bounded
input snapshot now requires a final `startxref` decimal locator that points
inside the preceding bytes to an `xref` table or a plausible PDF 1.5+
indirect `/Type /XRef` stream header. An ordinary indirect page object,
missing or malformed offset, wrong target and forged EOF-only stub refuse
before contact with CUPS. Native two-page paper and authored-ink PDF output
continues to pass the same check without any byte rewriting.

Five native Rust tests cover the admitted print workflow and added negative
cases. One test checks seven forged/truncated/repointed PDF trailer examples
against an executable fake-spool script, with no launch and unchanged input
bytes. An indirect xref-stream framing case remains eligible, without
claiming complete PDF parsing.

The indirect-stream preflight also now checks an exact PDF `/XRef` name
boundary rather than accepting `/XRefShadow` as an xref stream. An added
negative regression proves that the malformed type refuses both selected
file admission and fake-spool launch, preserving the source bytes. The
PDF grammar itself and earlier object contents are still not validated.

PDF version is now also checked for cross-reference *streams*: a stream
framing with PDF 1.0 or 1.4 in its header refuses, while the same minimal
framing under PDF 1.5 remains eligible. The negative regression first
failed against the old code, then passed with the additional restriction;
both refused cases are checked against fake CUPS invocation and original
file bytes. Legacy table-based PDF framing remains eligible across the
existing supported version range.

The bounded xref-stream header scan now also requires an actual outer
`<< ... >>` dictionary with an exact `/Type /XRef` name entry and a standalone
`stream` token followed by a line break. A red-before/green-after regression
rejects fake type names inside comments, strings, arrays, nested maps, or
outside the dictionary, plus incidental `downstream` and `%stream` text.
The same test admits nested dictionaries, comments, literal strings and CRLF
stream framing in a properly typed indirect stream. All six print tests and
strict all-target paper Clippy pass; fake CUPS remains untouched for refused
inputs and selected bytes remain unchanged.

The traditional `xref` locator now additionally demands at least one complete
fixed-width record in a numbered subsection, followed by a `trailer` and
`<< ... >>` dictionary. A red-before/green-after fixture had accepted a bare
`xref` keyword or partial table as printable; the corrected gate refuses
those forms, bad decimal records, wrong statuses and absent closing trailers
without CUPS side effects. Native two-page and multi-subsection table framing
remain admitted. All seven focused print tests and strict all-target Clippy
pass; this remains a bounded framing check, not a full PDF parser.

The PDF 1.5+ xref-stream path additionally requires a terminal
`endstream` keyword and `endobj` after the stream data, with only whitespace
or comments between the closing delimiters and `startxref`. A typed
xref-stream *header* without its two closing tokens previously passed the
printer preflight; six red-before/green-after fake-CUPS refusals now block
missing, fused or trailing-junk delimiters before spool submission. A
legitimate nested dictionary, CRLF header and terminal comment stay eligible.
This is still lexical framing, not parsing or decoding the stream payload.

The traditional xref-table trailer also now requires a lexical `/Size`
entry with a positive, unsigned decimal value. A valid native or compact
`<< /Size 1>>` spelling remains eligible, while absent, zero, signed,
non-decimal or duplicated size entries refuse before fake-CUPS invocation.
Five bad-trailer regressions first demonstrated previous acceptance, then
verified refusal without mutating the selected PDF. This does not inspect
all trailer entries, trust arbitrary PDF comments or parse full PDF syntax.

A further red-before/green-after preflight regression caught trailing
nontrivia tokens after a traditional trailer's `>>`: the prior scanner
stopped at its first closing delimiter and would admit a forged PDF to CUPS.
The bounded replacement checks outer dictionary closure, ignores nested
`/Size` names and words inside comments/literal strings, and permits only
PDF whitespace/comments before `startxref`.

Five added fake-table refusals and a valid nested/commented trailer now pass
beside the real native two-page PDF. Refused bytes remain unchanged, with no
fake-spool launch; no physical printer or full PDF grammar was exercised.

The bounded PDF dictionary scanners also now reject an unmatched
single `>` rather than silently skipping it. Two red-before cases showed
that otherwise plausible xref-stream headers and table trailers with stray
`>` could reach the fake spool. Paired unmatched `]` controls also refuse,
while native PDF tables and properly nested stream dictionaries remain admitted.

The table preflight now also bounds its advertised object indices:
trailer `/Size` must cover the exclusive upper endpoint of every complete
xref subsection. Previously a two-entry table with `/Size 1` passed, as
did a subsection beginning at object 100 with `/Size 100`. Both failed the
new red-before fake-spool tests and now refuse without modifying the PDF;
a valid `/Size 101` high-numbered subsection and real native pages still pass.

A fourth red-before printer admission regression showed that in-use
traditional xref table records with byte offsets of zero or 9,999,999,999
were accepted even for a tiny selected PDF. The bounded scanner now rejects
both outside-version-header and outside-file offsets for `n` records,
without constraining `f` records' free-object pointers. Two fake-CUPS
cases refuse without changing the selected PDF; real native two-page
export and ordinary synthetic table framing remain admitted. This check
does not validate object syntax at a claimed in-range byte offset.

An additional red-before regression showed that a table's in-use record
could name the `xref` keyword, an object's content instead of its header, or
a different object number/generation. The print gate now reads the declared
absolute byte position and requires a matching `number generation obj`
header before spool admission. Four malformed cases refuse without a CUPS
launch or source mutation, while exact native PDF pages and one synthetic
positive header stay admitted.

Free `f` entries remain exempt from the exact in-use header check. This
still does not parse indirect-object bodies, chained updates or complete
PDF syntax.

A follow-up red-before test caught a free `f` record referring to object
9,999,999,999 despite trailer `/Size 1`. The same bounded table scan now
includes every free-list next-object index in its trailer-size minimum,
and refuses generation counters above the PDF unsigned 16-bit maximum.
Two malformed tables refuse before fake CUPS contact; a two-entry free
chain and native exported pages still pass. This is not a verification of
complete free-list topology or an arbitrary PDF object grammar.

A further red-before fake-CUPS test admitted duplicate and overlapping
xref subsection object indices within one terminal table. The bounded,
allocation-free table gate now requires nonempty disjoint subsections in
increasing object-number order; sparse gaps remain eligible. Two
malformed subsection layouts refuse without CUPS side effects or source
mutations, and real native output retains its exact bytes. No claim is
made about incremental `/Prev` chains or full PDF reference validation.

A 24-MB-bounded selected PDF could also advertise `/Size 9999999999` on a
tiny terminal table; that formerly passed basic framing and would expose a
PDF consumer to an unbounded object count. A red-before fake-CUPS
regression now refuses sizes above the bounded record-equivalent ceiling
(1,333,334). The exact ceiling remains admitted, along with ordinary
native two-page output. This conservative resource policy may refuse
unusually sparse external PDFs, and does not decode stream records.

A red-before regression also exposed a cross-reference stream with no
advertised object count reaching the printer gate. Stream dictionaries now
require exactly one top-level direct decimal `/Size`, between 1 and the
existing 1,333,334-object input-budget ceiling. Ten malformed stream
fixtures refuse before fake CUPS can run, preserving selected bytes.

The shared numeric bound permits one plausible nested/commented stream
and the inclusive size ceiling while refusing oversized, signed, zero,
duplicated, malformed or nested-only `/Size` entries. These checks do not
decode stream records or establish whether PDF objects are complete.

Another red-before fake-CUPS regression found that plausible streams
could name object zero, an object number outside their own `/Size`, or a
generation above 65535. The indirect header now requires a positive,
in-range object identity and an unsigned 16-bit generation; six malformed
selected PDFs refuse with no spool launch or source-file mutation.

Three positive stream headers confirm legal object numbers, sparse IDs
and generation 65535 remain eligible. This still does not check object
cross-reference contents or establish a valid document root.

The stream preflight previously accepted an xref dictionary without
`/Length` and guessed where the data ended by scanning for `endstream`.
A red-before fake-CUPS regression now requires one direct, positive and
24-MB-bounded stream `/Length`, counting exact encoded payload bytes.

Nine malformed selected streams refuse with no spool launch or changed
source; four positive fixtures admit exact lengths and embedded literal
`endstream` text inside data. The final delimiter must follow those bytes
and a line break, not an incidental payload substring. Indirect `/Length`
references may require a reviewed re-export; no stream decoding occurs.

A traditional xref table could also declare object zero as an active
indirect object, or as free with an arbitrary generation counter.
A red-before fake-CUPS test confirmed this admission; the table gate now
reserves index zero for the permanent free head at generation 65535.

Two invalid selected PDFs refuse unchanged without fake spool contact.
An exact free-list head with a valid successor remains eligible, as do
native exported pages and sparse subsection tables. This does not build
or verify a complete free-list graph.

One more signed-count loophole in traditional xref subsection headers
allowed `+1` or `+0001` as a record count. A red-before fake-CUPS test
reproduced this admission; subsection counts now require nonempty ASCII
decimal digits, matching the already strict first-index parser.

Both forged count forms refuse unchanged before contacting the spooler.
Canonical unsigned counts, sparse sections and real native output remain
eligible. This does not check every aspect of PDF record line layout.

The traditional xref parser also previously combined a record's
offset, generation and `f`/`n` status from arbitrary separate lines,
or accepted unrelated tokens after the status. A red-before fake CUPS
regression proved both could be admitted by the word-oriented scanner.

Each xref record must now have exactly three horizontal-space-delimited
fields on one CR/LF-terminated line, starting after the subsection count's
own line break. Six malformed cases refuse without spooling or replacing
a selected PDF; both LF and CRLF valid records and native exported pages
remain accepted. This is lexical line framing, not cross-reference graph
reconstruction or full fixed-width PDF parsing.

A further stream admission gap allowed a typed xref stream without the
mandatory `/W` field-width array to reach fake CUPS. One red-before Rust
test now checks 13 absent, malformed, duplicate, nested-only and oversized
width declarations; all refuse unchanged before invoking the spooler.

Three direct width-triplet forms, including comments and a nested decoy,
remain eligible. Each width is an unsigned decimal from 0 to 8 bytes,
with at least one nonzero field; this is conservative local print framing.
It does not decompress or validate actual cross-reference stream entries.

An optional xref-stream `/Index` was previously accepted without
validating its object ranges, including out-of-size and overlapping entries.
Sixteen fake-CUPS refusal cases now reject empty, odd-length, signed,
zero-count, duplicated, nondecimal and overflowing index arrays unchanged.

The bounded parser accepts five omitted or valid direct range forms,
including PDF comments, sparse gaps and nested decoys. Direct ranges must
be increasing, disjoint and within `/Size` under the conservative local
policy; the stream payload itself is still not decoded.

A further positive regression constructs a genuine PDF 1.5 single-page
Letter document with catalog, page tree, page and binary cross-reference
stream objects. `pdfinfo` independently recognized its one physical page
and version without repair diagnostics; the native `--dry-run` accepted
its exact 353-byte snapshot without contacting any queue.

The Rust fake-CUPS test additionally admits explicit, implicit and
partitioned `/Index` forms with real 35-byte rows, and verifies the
reviewed digest, unchanged source and byte-identical spool input for each
variant. This confirms compatibility beyond synthetic framing fixtures;
no physical printing or full PDF semantic validation is claimed.

The genuine cross-reference-stream compatibility fixture now also
selects an independently read PDF 2.0 one-page document: dry-run review
and exact fake-CUPS bytes remain admissible. Replacing only the header
with PDF 1.4 is intentionally refused before fake spooling, even when a
permissive viewer can repair that out-of-version stream. This keeps the
local print policy aligned with PDF 1.5's stream introduction.

The check is a restrictive framing preflight,
not a trustworthy PDF syntax/content validator or printer acceptance proof.
The task stays open for real-device alignment and complete saved overlays.

## Terminal table self-reference refusal (partial) — 2026-11-04

The selected-PDF preflight now rejects a traditional in-use xref record whose
absolute offset points into its own terminal cross-reference table or trailer.
Previously a forged trailer literal containing `1 0 obj` could impersonate
an earlier indirect object and reach CUPS despite no actual object at that
location. A red-before/green-after Rust fake-CUPS regression proves that the
forged selection now refuses without process launch or source-file mutation.

The complete native two-page PDF and all other supported print fixtures
remain eligible. The 23-test printer suite and strict all-target print
Clippy pass. This is still bounded lexical framing, not a PDF object-graph
parser, a real queue test or proof of physical print alignment.

## Escaped PDF dictionary-key parity (partial) — 2026-11-04

PDF names can contain `#`-encoded bytes: `/S#69ze` is another spelling
of `/Size`. Previously the local printer gate treated those as unrelated
keys, admitting duplicate xref table or stream `/Size` declarations whose
later decoded value could bypass the 24-MB resource-count ceiling.

A red-before/green-after fake-CUPS regression now verifies duplicate
escaped `/Size` refusal on both forms without changing source bytes or
contacting CUPS. The same bounded allocation-free name comparison detects
escaped duplicate `/Type`, `/W`, `/Index` and `/Length` stream keys. Single
encoded names are still supported, including `/X#52ef` for `/XRef`.

Native paper output, genuine PDF 1.5/2.0 stream fixtures, 24 print tests
and strict all-target Clippy pass. No PDF decompression, full grammar or
physical printer acceptance is implied.

## Bounded incremental xref predecessor check (partial) — 2026-11-04

A traditional terminal xref table now checks an optional top-level `/Prev`
entry before CUPS submission. Its single direct decimal byte offset must
point backward before the terminal table, to a previous xref-table token or
an admitted PDF 1.5+ xref-stream header. Duplicate aliases such as
`/Prev` and `/P#72ev`, zero, signed, nonnumeric, malformed, forward and
non-xref pointers refuse without sending bytes or modifying the PDF.
Nested metadata is not mistaken for a top-level predecessor.

The red-before/green-after fake-CUPS test appends an incremental revision
to the genuine native two-page PDF and verifies that correct direct and
escaped `/Prev` fields spool exactly the selected bytes, while malformed
revisions never reach the fake queue. Independent `pdfinfo` read both the
original and incrementally revised A4 PDF as two pages with no errors.

This inspects only the immediate prior xref header, not a complete chain of
PDF revisions, object references or contents; no real CUPS device or
physical alignment is verified. The broader printed-page task stays open.

## Terminal cross-reference stream predecessor check (partial) — 2026-11-04

The optional `/Prev` pointer in a terminal PDF 1.5+ xref stream now receives
its own bounded admission check, matching the traditional trailer policy.
Previously a stream could name offset zero, a forward offset, a forged
object interior or duplicate encoded and unencoded `/Prev` names, and still
reach the selected local CUPS spooler. A red-before/green-after fake-CUPS
test now proves each is refused without opening the spooler or mutating the
selected PDF.

A genuine PDF 1.5 one-page xref-stream PDF with an appended stream revision
remains admissible with correct direct or PDF-name-escaped previous offsets,
including nested metadata decoys. Accepted jobs retain their exact input
bytes and reviewed SHA-256 digest. This checks the immediate predecessor
header only; it does not recursively validate prior revisions, decode xref
stream rows, or demonstrate physical printing.

## Framed immediate predecessor table record (partial) — 2026-11-04

A `/Prev` pointer to a traditional cross-reference table can no longer
qualify solely by naming the bytes `xref` followed by whitespace. A new
bounded, nonrecursive probe requires a positive in-range subsection count
and a complete first record with exact decimal field widths, a valid
16-bit generation, and a legal object-zero free head when applicable.

Three red-before/green-after incremental PDF selections demonstrate that
an earlier fake `xref` keyword, an incomplete subsection and a malformed
record no longer reach fake CUPS. All are refused without modifying the
selected PDF; the genuine native earlier xref and an earlier PDF 1.5+
stream header remain eligible. The gate does not validate the remainder
of previous xref tables, their trailers or full incremental chains.

## Framed immediate predecessor stream body (partial) — 2026-11-04

The earlier xref-stream target of a terminal `/Prev` pointer must now have
not only a plausible indirect `/Type /XRef` header, but also the exact
announced `/Length` bytes and standalone `endstream` and `endobj` framing.
The same bounded byte-frame routine serves the terminal xref-stream gate;
only terminal streams require no subsequent nontrivia bytes before
`startxref`, since earlier revisions have their own older trailer.

Three red-before/green-after fake-CUPS regressions refuse a header with no
stream data, a missing `endobj`, and a fused `endobjjunk` marker. All leave
the selected PDF unchanged and never invoke the fake queue. The genuine
PDF 1.5 prior stream and native two-page PDF remain admitted. This does not
parse stream rows, authenticate older object graphs or certify a printer.

## Acceptance decision

Open. Provide the actual bounded Rust/backend or browser integration and an
accepted/refused example before marking the executable capability complete.
Theoretical design acceptance alone cannot establish that this feature runs.

## Explicit named local printing (partial) — 2026-11-03

The separate Rust `atrament-print` executable submits an already-exported,
operator-selected PDF to one explicitly selected local CUPS queue. It reads
a single regular file through the existing Linux symlink-refusing file
opener and exact bounded reader; missing/invalid selections, queue names,
PDF framing and 24 MB limit all refuse before invoking `lp`. The submission
has no shell, no implicit printer selection, and sends the exact validated
PDF bytes over standard input rather than reopening a mutable filename.

The standalone `atrament-profiles` notebook exporter already honors
`printed_sheet` on preprinted paper, retains any optional numeric date,
page numbering, border and explicit authored strokes, and omits existing
ruling. Exports still never enqueue printer jobs. The native `atrament-print`
command is the deliberately separate physical-print intent.

**Evidence:** three focused Rust tests use a real two-page, unruled native
PDF and an isolated fake `lp` executable to verify the complete exact byte
stream and printer arguments without contacting a physical printer.
See [local print CLI](../../../technical/local-print-cli.md) for usage and
scope. No real CUPS queue, print alignment or successful physical page
has been verified, and no saved image overlays exist. This executable
task remains **open** pending those criteria.
